# **13. Viva Questions and Answers**

These questions are especially important for this practical.

**Q1. What is a structure in Solidity?**

A structure is a user-defined data type that groups different variables under one name.

**Q2. What is an array?**

An array is a collection of elements stored under one variable name.

**Q3. Why do we use structures in this program?**

We use structures to store student details such as roll number, name and marks together.

**Q4. Why do we use arrays?**

We use arrays to store multiple student records.

**Q5. What is a fallback function?**

A fallback function is a special function that executes when an unknown function is called or when Ether is sent without function data and no receive function is defined.

**Q6. What is the difference between fallback and receive?**

| Fallback                     | Receive                     |
| ---------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Executes for unmatched calls | Executes for empty calldata |
| Can accept Ether if payable  | Must be payable             |
| Written as `fallback()`      | Written as `receive()`      |

**Q7. What is&#x20;**`push()`**&#x20;in Solidity?**

It is used to add a new element to the end of a dynamic array.

**Q8. What is&#x20;**`memory`**?**

Memory is temporary data storage that exists only during function execution.

**Q9. What is&#x20;**`storage`**?**

Storage is permanent data storage maintained on the blockchain.

**Q10. What is&#x20;**`msg.sender`**?**

It represents the address of the account or contract that calls the current function.

**Q11. What is&#x20;**`msg.value`**?**

It represents the amount of Ether sent with the current transaction, in Wei.

**Q12. What is an event in Solidity?**

An event is used to record information in transaction logs so that external applications can monitor contract activities.

**Q13. What is gas used?**

It is the actual amount of computational gas consumed by a transaction.

**Q14. What is a transaction fee?**

It is the amount of Ether paid to process a blockchain transaction.

**Q15. Why does adding student data require gas?**

Because adding student data modifies the blockchain's permanent storage.

**Q16. Does displaying student data require gas?**

A normal read-only call does not require gas payment. However, executing it as an on-chain transaction does consume gas.

**Q17. What is the smallest unit of Ether?**

Wei.

\\[ 1 ETH = 10^{18} Wei \\]

**Q18. What is the purpose of Remix IDE?**

Remix is used to write, compile, test and deploy Solidity smart contracts.

**Q19. Can we delete student data from the blockchain?**

In this program, we have not written any delete function. Blockchain storage changes require specially written functions, and deleting data does not necessarily erase historical blockchain records.

**Q20. Why do we use a test network?**

To test smart contracts without spending real cryptocurrency.

